Package: looksee
Version: 0.2.2
Architecture: all
Section: graphics
Priority: optional
Depends: python3 (>= 3.10), python3-gi, gir1.2-gtk-4.0, gir1.2-adw-1, libadwaita-1-0
Maintainer: Kris Forbes <krisf@users.noreply.github.com>
Description: fast dark single-window image viewer
 Looksee opens an image, flips through the containing folder with arrow
 keys or the headerbar buttons, zooms (fit / 1:1 / 0.1x-8x, Ctrl+wheel),
 goes fullscreen with F11, and loops the folder as a slideshow on a
 configurable interval (default 5 s, optional 220 ms crossfade).
 .
 Compact GTK4 / libadwaita application; settings live in
 ~/.config/looksee/config.json.
 .
 A look-ahead cache pre-renders the three pictures around the current
 one (two forward, one back) under a hard 200 MB budget, so next and
 previous land instantly even in huge folders.
 .
 0.2.0 (FTR2): true-black OLED UI; slideshow orientation filter
 (all / landscape / portrait); the headerbar auto-hides 5 s into a
 slideshow and returns on any mouse activity.
 .
 0.2.1 (FTR3): every storage read — folder enumeration, image decode,
 file metadata, orientation probes — is asynchronous, so on very slow
 mounts (network shares, rclone/restic FUSE) the window always stays
 responsive; pictures and listings simply arrive when the mount
 answers, and the previous slide stays visible until the next one is
 ready.
 .
 0.2.2 (FTR5): large images open fast — each picture first decodes at
 viewport resolution (a 48-megapixel JPEG costs a few MB of RAM instead
 of ~200 MB, so the look-ahead cache now holds a dozen of them), and
 zooming in past that detail — or growing the window — re-decodes in the
 background at true resolution, bounded so memory stays under control.
